WEBVTT

resistance-05-1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:08.310
Previously: order has no heart, but it
has many hands. What it fears is refusal.

resistance-05-2
00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:15.623
This time, we take a step back: why would
someone refuse an identity assigned to

resistance-05-3
00:00:15.623 --> 00:00:16.080
them?

resistance-05-4
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.990
Episode 5: Assigned Identity

resistance-05-5
00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:28.748
Back to the street dance club. On the day
the new member arrives, she receives a

resistance-05-6
00:00:28.748 --> 00:00:29.880
ready-made card.

resistance-05-7
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:34.157
The card says: new member—stand in the
back row, practice the basics first, and

resistance-05-8
00:00:34.157 --> 00:00:38.380
dance in the club’s style. Who, where,
and how to act are all written for her.

resistance-05-9
00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:45.365
This card was not written just for her.
It describes what a ‘new member’ should

resistance-05-10
00:00:45.365 --> 00:00:47.310
be—a social narrative.

resistance-05-11
00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:53.267
But her own experience is different: she
has practiced another kind of dance since

resistance-05-12
00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:56.880
childhood, for eight years, and performed
onstage many times.

resistance-05-13
00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.575
The card tells a social narrative; what
she has lived is personal experience.

resistance-05-14
00:01:01.575 --> 00:01:04.990
They do not match, and dissatisfaction
grows in the gap.

resistance-05-15
00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:08.540
The author’s proposition

resistance-05-16
00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:14.121
The street dance club is just a small
example. The author’s draft gives a

resistance-05-17
00:01:14.121 --> 00:01:14.980
weightier one:

resistance-05-18
00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:19.564
A patriotic gay citizen living under
anti-LGBT policies may become

resistance-05-19
00:01:19.564 --> 00:01:22.620
dissatisfied with the identity of
‘citizen.’

resistance-05-20
00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:27.814
He has not stopped loving his country.
The ‘citizen’ identity assigned to him

resistance-05-21
00:01:27.814 --> 00:01:30.310
leaves no place for someone like him.

resistance-05-22
00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:33.779
Author’s OSIPP draft, 2B(i): ‘A patriotic
gay citizen under anti-LGBT policies may

resistance-05-23
00:01:33.779 --> 00:01:35.640
become dissatisfied with national
identity.’

resistance-05-24
00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:42.620
Resentment at being misrecognized makes
her want to reject the card.

resistance-05-25
00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:47.426
But people become ‘someone’ through
social recognition. Throwing away the

resistance-05-26
00:01:47.426 --> 00:01:52.100
card will not make her freer—it will only
make her invisible to others.

resistance-05-27
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:54.287
Author’s OSIPP draft, 2B(i): subjects are
constituted through social recognition

resistance-05-28
00:01:54.287 --> 00:01:56.420
(the author cites Butler, The Psychic
Life of Power, 1997, pp. 20–21), so they

resistance-05-29
00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.553
are compelled to seek an alternative
identity, both for self-understanding and

resistance-05-30
00:01:58.553 --> 00:01:58.990
for recognition.

resistance-05-31
00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:06.990
So she can only do something else: draw a
card of her own.

resistance-05-32
00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:11.013
‘Someone who knows another dance.’
‘Someone who wants to choreograph her

resistance-05-33
00:02:11.013 --> 00:02:14.980
own.’ A few others who also feel
misunderstood gather around this card.

resistance-05-34
00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:21.394
A dissenting group: creating a satisfying
self-image in order to reject an identity

resistance-05-35
00:02:21.394 --> 00:02:24.640
that makes its members feel
misunderstood.

resistance-05-36
00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:32.626
Pause for a moment: is there an identity
others have given you that has always

resistance-05-37
00:02:32.626 --> 00:02:35.010
felt like ‘That is not me’?

resistance-05-38
00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:38.779
Guardrail 1: dissatisfaction does not
mean being right. An assigned identity is

resistance-05-39
00:02:38.779 --> 00:02:41.690
not necessarily wrong; a self-image is
not necessarily right.

resistance-05-40
00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:45.522
Guardrail 2: where do the cards come
from? This series argues that the

resistance-05-41
00:02:45.522 --> 00:02:49.245
material people use to understand
themselves is itself regulated and

resistance-05-42
00:02:49.245 --> 00:02:50.230
produced by power.

resistance-05-43
00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.522
Author’s OSIPP draft, 2B(i): the material
subjects use for self-understanding is

resistance-05-44
00:02:52.522 --> 00:02:54.757
regulated and produced by power (the
author cites Butler 1997, pp. 2–3). Here,

resistance-05-45
00:02:54.757 --> 00:02:56.620
‘power’ means the dynamic form of the
political order as a whole.

resistance-05-46
00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.560
This episode’s comparison card (take a
screenshot)

resistance-05-47
00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.482
Assigned identity — ‘New member: stand in
the back row; dance in the club’s style.’

resistance-05-48
00:03:04.482 --> 00:03:05.380
A social narrative.

resistance-05-49
00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.428
Where dissatisfaction comes from — The
card versus her eight years of dancing.

resistance-05-50
00:03:08.428 --> 00:03:10.460
Social narrative does not match personal
experience.

resistance-05-51
00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.589
She cannot simply throw it away — Without
the card, others cannot see her. People

resistance-05-52
00:03:13.589 --> 00:03:14.980
are constituted through recognition.

resistance-05-53
00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:21.864
Dissenting group — The card ‘we wrote
ourselves.’ Creating a satisfying

resistance-05-54
00:03:21.864 --> 00:03:22.930
self-image.

resistance-05-55
00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:27.450
She now has a card she is satisfied with.

resistance-05-56
00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:32.390
The problem is: this card still has no
place in the club.

resistance-05-57
00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:36.180
The chair is still there.

resistance-05-58
00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.840
Next time:

resistance-05-59
00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:44.920
To be recognized, you must first learn
the other side’s language.

resistance-05-60
00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:52.130
This is UjiBricks.
